1) Magnitude spectrum (log-scale)
The code uses FFT to convert the image into the frequency domain. 
The log-scale magnitude spectrum makes low- and high-frequency components easier to see. 
Bright areas represent stronger frequency components.

2) Ideal & Gaussian Low-pass in frequency
The code creates two low-pass filters. 
The ideal filter sharply removes frequencies outside a selected radius, while the Gaussian filter gradually reduces higher frequencies.

3) Apply LP filters in frequency domain
The code multiplies the image's Fourier transform by each low-pass filter and then applies inverse FFT.
The resulting images are smoother because high-frequency details and noise are reduced.

4) High-pass via complement
The code creates high-pass filters by subtracting the low-pass filters from 1.
This removes low-frequency components and preserves high-frequency details, making edges and fine structures more visible.

5) Compare spatial vs frequency-domain Gaussian LP
The code compares Gaussian smoothing in the spatial domain with Gaussian filtering in the frequency domain. 
Both produce similar smoothing effects, although small differences can occur due to padding, filter implementation and boundary handling.

6) Reflection answers:
1. Why does ideal LP cause ringing (Gibbs phenomenon)?
An ideal low-pass filter has a sharp cutoff in the frequency domain.
Its spatial impulse response extends with oscillations causing ringing around sharp edges.

2. What does fftshift do visually?
fftshift moves the zero-frequency component to the center of the spectrum which makes the low-frequency components easier to visualize.

3. When is frequency-domain filtering computationally preferable?
It is useful for large kernels, repeated filtering, and operations involving frequency masks.
